Part 1: every bit pattern decoded by NVIDIA's conversion functions
FP16       65536 patterns:  63488 finite,   2 infinite, 2046 NaN; largest finite 65504        smallest positive 5.960464e-08
BF16       65536 patterns:  65280 finite,   2 infinite,  254 NaN; largest finite 3.389531e+38 smallest positive 9.18355e-41
FP8 E4M3     256 patterns:    254 finite,   0 infinite,    2 NaN; largest finite 448          smallest positive 0.001953125
FP8 E5M2     256 patterns:    248 finite,   2 infinite,    6 NaN; largest finite 57344        smallest positive 1.525879e-05

Part 2: our rounding model against NVIDIA's float -> format conversion
FP16      model agrees with NVIDIA's conversion on 1000000 of 1000000 values; largest relative error 0.000487805 (u = 2^-11 = 0.000488281)
BF16      model agrees with NVIDIA's conversion on 1000000 of 1000000 values; largest relative error 0.00389069 (u = 2^-8 = 0.00390625)
FP8 E4M3  model agrees with NVIDIA's conversion on 1000000 of 1000000 values; largest relative error 0.0588229 (u = 2^-4 = 0.0625)
FP8 E5M2  model agrees with NVIDIA's conversion on 1000000 of 1000000 values; largest relative error 0.11111 (u = 2^-3 = 0.125)
TF32      no host conversion in this toolkit's headers was used; the model (p = 11, FP32 exponent range) is not checked here

Part 3: three values through each conversion (FP8 conversions saturate to the largest finite value)
x = 3.1415927   FP16 3.140625     BF16 3.140625     E4M3 3.25       E5M2 3         
x = 70000       FP16 inf          BF16 70144        E4M3 448        E5M2 57344     
x = 9.9999999e-09 FP16 0            BF16 1.0011718e-08 E4M3 0          E5M2 0         
